"Command-line interface / Commands - Composer (https://getcomposer.org/doc/03-cli.md)\nciteturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view1\",\"pattern\":\"prefer-lowest\"}); Total lines: 1015\nL228:   * --prefer-stable: Prefer stable versions of dependencies. Can also be set via the COMPOSER_PREFER_STABLE=1 env var.\nL229:   * --prefer-lowest: Prefer lowest versions of dependencies. Useful for testing minimal versions of requirements, generally used with `--prefer-stable`. Can also be set via the COMPOSER_PREFER_LOWEST=1 env var.\nL271:   * --prefer-stable: Prefer stable versions of dependencies. Can also be set via the COMPOSER_PREFER_STABLE=1 env var.\nL272:   * --prefer-lowest: Prefer lowest versions of dependencies. Useful for testing minimal versions of requirements, generally used with `--prefer-stable`. Can also be set via the COMPOSER_PREFER_LOWEST=1 env var.\nL987: ### COMPOSER_PREFER_LOWESTcite94†# L988: \nL989: If set to `1`, it is the equivalent of passing the `--prefer-lowest` option to `update` or `require`.\nL990: ### COMPOSER_PREFER_DEV_OVER_PRERELEASEcite95†# L991: \nL992: If set to `1`, when resolving dependencies with both `--prefer-stable` and `--prefer-lowest` enabled, dev versions are treated as more stable than alpha/beta/RC versions in cases where no stable release exists. This is useful to test lowest versions while still preferring branches that may contain critical fixes over prerelease versions.\nL993: ### COMPOSER_MINIMAL_CHANGEScite96†# L994: \nL995: If set to `1`, it is the equivalent of passing the `--minimal-changes` option to `update`, `require` or `remove`.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCommand-line interface / Commands - Composer (https://getcomposer.org/doc/03-cli.md)\nciteturn1view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view1\",\"pattern\":\"ignores config.platform\"}); Total lines: 1015\nL336: ## check-platform-reqscite15†# L337: \nL338: The check-platform-reqs command checks that your PHP and extensions versions match the platform requirements of the installed packages. This can be used to verify that a production server has all the extensions needed to run a project after installing it for example.\nL339: \nL340: Unlike update/install, this command will ignore config.platform settings and check the real platform packages so you can be certain you have the required platform dependencies.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRepositories - Composer (https://getcomposer.org/doc/05-repositories.md)\nciteturn1view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view2\",\"pattern\":\"root package\"}); Total lines: 630\nL45: ### Repositorycite7†# L46: \nL47: A repository is a package source. It's a list of packages/versions. Composer will look in all your repositories to find the packages your project requires.\nL48: \nL49: By default, only the Packagist.org repository is registered in Composer. You can add more repositories to your project by declaring them in `composer.json`.\nL50: \nL51: Repositories are only available to the root package and the repositories defined in your dependencies will not be loaded. Read the cite32†FAQ entry if you want to learn why.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nContexts reference - GitHub Docs (https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/contexts)\nciteturn1view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view5\",\"pattern\":\"workflow_sha\"}); Total lines: 1224\nL512: `job.workflow_sha`  | `string`  | The commit SHA of the workflow file that defines the current job. (not available on GitHub Enterprise Server)\nL513: `job.workflow_repository`  | `string`  | The `owner/repo` of the repository containing the workflow file that defines the current job. For example, `octo-org/octo-repo`. (not available on GitHub Enterprise Server)\nL516: This example `job` context uses a PostgreSQL service container with mapped ports. If there are no containers or service containers used in a job, the `job` context only contains `status`. The `check_run_id` and workflow identity properties (`workflow_ref`, `workflow_sha`, `workflow_repository`, `workflow_file_path`) are not available on GitHub Enterprise Server.\nL517: \nL518:     `{\nL519:       \"status\": \"success\",\nL520:       \"check_run_id\": 51725241954,\nL521:       \"workflow_ref\": \"octo-org/octo-repo/.github/workflows/deploy.yml@refs/heads/main\",\nL522:       \"workflow_sha\": \"abc123def456789abc123def456789abc123def4\",\nL523:       \"workflow_repository\": \"octo-org/octo-repo\",\nL524:       \"workflow_file_path\": \".github/workflows/deploy.yml\",\nL525:       \"container\": {\nL526:         \"network\": \"github_network_53269bd575974817b43f4733536b200c\"\nL527:       },\nL528:       \"services\": {\nL529:         \"postgres\": {\nL530:           \"id\": \"60972d9aa486605e66b0dad4abb638dc3d9116f566579e418166eedb8abb9105\",\nL531:           \"ports\": {\nL532:             \"5432\": \"49153\"\nL533:           },\nL534:           \"network\": \"github_network_53269bd575974817b43f4733536b200c\"\nL535:         }\nL536:       }\nL537:     }\nL538:     `\nL539: ### cite54†Example usage of the `job` context L540: \nL541: This example workflow configures a PostgreSQL service container, and automatically maps port 5432 in the service container to a randomly chosen available port on the host. The `job` context is used to access the number of the port that was assigned on the host.\nL558:         steps:\nL559:           - run: pg_isready -h localhost -p ${{ job.services.postgres.ports[5432] }}\nL560:           - run: echo \"Run tests against Postgres\"\nL561: \nL562: \nL574:             ports:\nL575:               # Maps TCP port 5432 in the service container to a randomly chosen available port on the host.\nL576:               - 5432\nL577: \nL578:         steps:\nL579:           - run: pg_isready -h localhost -p ${{ job.services.postgres.ports[5432] }}\nL580:           - run: echo \"Run tests against Postgres\"\nL581:     `\nL582: ### cite55†Example usage of `job` context workflow identity L583: \nL584: Note\nL585: \nL586: The `job.workflow_*` context properties are not available on GitHub Enterprise Server.\nL587: \nL588: This example reusable workflow uses `job.workflow_repository` and `job.workflow_sha` to check out its own source code, rather than the caller's repository. This is useful when a reusable workflow needs to access files co-located with the workflow definition.\nL589: YAML\nL590: \nL591:     # In a reusable workflow (e.g., octo-org/shared-workflows/.github/workflows/deploy.yml)\nL592:     name: Reusable deploy workflow\nL593:     on:\nL594:       workflow_call:\nL595: \nL596:     jobs:\nL597:       deploy:\nL598:         runs-on: ubuntu-latest\nL599:         steps:\nL600:           - uses: actions/checkout@v6\nL601:             with:\nL602:               repository: ${{ job.workflow_repository }}\nL603:               ref: ${{ job.workflow_sha }}\nL604: \nL605:           - run: echo \"Deploying from ${{ job.workflow_ref }}\"\nL606:           - run: echo \"Workflow file path is ${{ job.workflow_file_path }}\"\nL607: \nL608: \nL609:     `# In a reusable workflow (e.g., octo-org/shared-workflows/.github/workflows/deploy.yml)\nL610:     name: Reusable deploy workflow\nL611:     on:\nL612:       workflow_call:\nL613: \nL614:     jobs:\nL615:       deploy:\nL616:         runs-on: ubuntu-latest\nL617:         steps:\nL618:           - uses: actions/checkout@v6\nL619:             with:\nL620:               repository: ${{ job.workflow_repository }}\nL621:               ref: ${{ job.workflow_sha }}\nL622:           - run: echo \"Deploying from ${{ job.workflow_ref }}\"\nL623:           - run: echo \"Workflow file path is ${{ job.workflow_file_path }}\"\nL624:     `\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nImmutable releases - GitHub Docs (https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/concepts/supply-chain-security/immutable-releases)\nciteturn1view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view6\",\"pattern\":\"draft\"}); Total lines: 57\nL39: ## cite6†Best practices for publishing immutable releases L40: \nL41: We recommend you use the following workflow for publishing an immutable release.\nL42: \nL43:   1. Create the release as a draft.\nL44:   2. Attach all associated assets to the draft release.\nL45:   3. Publish the draft release.\nL46: \nL47: This ensures that all assets are in place before the release becomes immutable, preventing the need to work around immutability restrictions. For more information, see cite8†Managing releases in a repository .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSemantic Versioning 2.0.0 | Semantic Versioning (https://semver.org/)\nciteturn1view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view7\",\"pattern\":\"MUST NOT be modified\"}); Total lines: 224\nL62: ## Semantic Versioning Specification (SemVer)\nL63: \nL64: The key words “MUST”, “MUST NOT”, “REQUIRED”, “SHALL”, “SHALL NOT”, “SHOULD”, “SHOULD NOT”, “RECOMMENDED”, “MAY”, and “OPTIONAL” in this document are to be interpreted as described in cite43†RFC 2119†tools.ietf.org .\nL65: \nL66:   1. Software using Semantic Versioning MUST declare a public API. This API could be declared in the code itself or exist strictly in documentation. However it is done, it SHOULD be precise and comprehensive.\nL67:   2. A normal version number MUST take the form X.Y.Z where X, Y, and Z are non-negative integers, and MUST NOT contain leading zeroes. X is the major version, Y is the minor version, and Z is the patch version. Each element MUST increase numerically. For instance: 1.9.0 -> 1.10.0 -> 1.11.0.\nL68: \nL69:   3. Once a versioned package has been released, the contents of that version MUST NOT be modified. Any modifications MUST be released as a new version.\nL70:   4. Major version zero (0.y.z) is for initial development. Anything MAY change at any time. The public API SHOULD NOT be considered stable.\nL71: \nL72:   5. Version 1.0.0 defines the public API. The way in which the version number is incremented after this release is dependent on this public API and how it changes.\nL73: \nL74:   6. Patch version Z (x.y.Z | x > 0) MUST be incremented if only backward compatible bug fixes are introduced. A bug fix is defined as an internal change that fixes incorrect behavior.\nL75:   7. Minor version Y (x.Y.z | x > 0) MUST be incremented if new, backward compatible functionality is introduced to the public API. It MUST be incremented if any public API functionality is marked as deprecated. It MAY be incremented if substantial new functionality or improvements are introduced within the private code. It MAY include patch level changes. Patch version MUST be reset to 0 when minor version is incremented.\nL76:   8. Major version X (X.y.z | X > 0) MUST be incremented if any backward incompatible changes are introduced to the public API. It MAY also include minor and patch level changes. Patch and minor versions MUST be reset to 0 when major version is incremented.\nL77:   9. A pre-release version MAY be denoted by appending a hyphen and a series of dot separated identifiers immediately following the patch version. Identifiers MUST comprise only ASCII alphanumerics and hyphens [0-9A-Za-z-]. Identifiers MUST NOT be empty. Numeric identifiers MUST NOT include leading zeroes. Pre-release versions have a lower precedence than the associated normal version.\nL78: A pre-release version indicates that the version is unstable and might not satisfy the intended compatibility requirements as denoted by its associated normal version. Examples: 1.0.0-alpha, 1.0.0-alpha.1, 1.0.0-0.3.7, 1.0.0-x.7.z.92, 1.0.0-x-y-z.--.\nL79:   10. Build metadata MAY be denoted by appending a plus sign and a series of dot separated identifiers immediately following the patch or pre-release version. Identifiers MUST comprise only ASCII alphanumerics and hyphens [0-9A-Za-z-]. Identifiers MUST NOT be empty. Build metadata MUST be ignored when determining version precedence. Thus two versions that differ only in the build metadata, have the same precedence.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDatabase: Migrations | Laravel 13.x - The clean stack for Artisans and agents (https://laravel.com/docs/13.x/migrations)\nciteturn1view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view9\",\"pattern\":\"pretend\"}); Redirected to URL: https://laravel.com/framework/docs/13.x/migrations; Total lines: 2438\nL422: If you would like to see the SQL statements that will be executed by the migrations without actually running them, you may provide the `--pretend` flag to the `migrate` command:\nL423: \nL424:     `\nL425: \nL426:     1 php artisan migrate --pretend\nL427: \nL428: \nL429:     php artisan migrate --pretend\nL430: \nL431:     `\nL490: If you would like to see the SQL statements that will be executed by the migrations without actually running them, you may provide the `--pretend` flag to the `migrate:rollback` command:\nL491: \nL492:     `\nL493: \nL494:     1 php artisan migrate:rollback --pretend\nL495: \nL496: \nL497:     php artisan migrate:rollback --pretend\nL498: \nL499:     `\nL500: \nL501: The `migrate:reset` command will roll back all of your application's migrations:\nL502: \nL503:     `\nL504: \nL505:     1 php artisan migrate:reset\nL506: \nL507: \nL508:     php artisan migrate:reset\nL509: \nL510:     `\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAuthorization - OWASP Cheat Sheet Series (https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\nciteturn1view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view10\",\"pattern\":\"Deny by Default\"}); Total lines: 334\nL212: ### Deny by Defaultcite20†¶ L213: Even when no access control rules are explicitly matched, the application cannot remain neutral when an entity is requesting access to a particular resource. The application must always make a decision, whether implicitly or explicitly, to either deny or permit the requested access. Logic errors and other mistakes relating to access control may happen, especially when access requirements are complex; consequently, one should not rely entirely on explicitly defined rules for matching all possible requests.\nL214: For security purposes an application should be configured to deny access by default.\nL215: Consider the following points and best practices:\nL216:   * Adopt a \"deny-by-default\" mentality both during initial development and whenever new functionality or resources are exposed by the app. One should be able to explicitly justify why a specific permission was granted to a particular user or group rather than assuming access to be the default position.\nL217:   * Although some frameworks or libraries may themselves adopt a deny-by-default strategy, explicit configuration should be preferred over relying on framework or library defaults. The logic and defaults of third-party code may evolve over time, without the developer's full knowledge or understanding of the change's implications for a particular project.\nL218: ### Validate the Permissions on Every Requestcite21†¶ L219: Permission should be validated correctly on every request, regardless of whether the request was initiated by an AJAX script, server-side, or any other source. The technology used to perform such checks should allow for global, application-wide configuration rather than needing to be applied individually to every method or class. Remember an attacker only needs to find one way in. Even if just a single access control check is \"missed\", the confidentiality and/or integrity of a resource can be jeopardized.\n"
